// A blocking call blocks only the worker that makes it: tasks on other
// workers keep running (spec/04-semantics.md, Suspend Points). A worker
// waits for ever in Server.accept() on a port that no program connects to.
// The main task, on another worker, keeps running, and the program ends
// when the main task ends. An implementation that blocks every worker in a
// blocking call never prints the second line and hits the timeout.
//
// The CHECK lines do not bind the bootstrap interpreter. It runs every
// worker on one host thread under the implementation-gated exception of
// spec/04-semantics.md, rule 2: "A blocking call blocks every worker, not
// only its own." The probe on the NOT-BINDING line holds the bootstrap to
// that rule in place of this one.
// NOT-BINDING: bootstrap bootstrap-only/one_thread_blocking_call.lox
fun acceptor(main) {
    var server = listen("127.0.0.1", 0);
    task.send(main, "listening");
    server.accept();
    task.send(main, "accepted");
}

task.spawn_worker(acceptor, task.self());
print task.receive();
sleep(0.2);
print "main runs while the worker waits in accept";

// STATUS: ok
// TIMEOUT: 20
// CHECK: listening
// CHECK: main runs while the worker waits in accept
